# Line endings belong to the repository, not to whoever cloned it.
#
# Until this file existed, the only thing keeping the index pure LF was one
# machine's `core.autocrlf=true`. A clone without it would have committed CRLF,
# and scripted edits make that likely rather than theoretical: writing a file
# from Python rewrites every line ending in it, so a one-line change can arrive
# as a whole-file diff.
#
# `eol=lf` covers the working tree deliberately, and it does override
# `core.autocrlf`. Matching bytes on both sides is the point: a stray rewrite
# then shows up as a diff instead of hiding behind the clean filter. Before this
# landed, 89 tracked files were CRLF on disk against LF blobs and six more held
# a mix of both within one file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception, for files that do not exist here yet. CMD.EXE mis-parses
# LF-only labels and multi-line `if` blocks, so a `.bat` checked out with LF is
# broken in a way that is hard to see. Declaring it now means adding one later
# cannot break it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Belt and braces. `text=auto` already classifies these correctly - it looks for
# a NUL byte in the first 8000 - but saying so explicitly means the answer never
# depends on what happens to sit in a file's header.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zst binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.whl binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary

# Source-generated FastAPI templates retain their exact response whitespace.
engine-rust/src/http/documentation-assets/*.html whitespace=-blank-at-eol,-blank-at-eof
